  Reference: camera.background_color '#000000'; vmobject {'default_stroke_width': 4.0, 'default_stroke_color': '#DDDDDD', 'default_fill_color': '#888888'}
  Portal:    camera.background_color {} (missing key); vmobject {}

The portal's initialize_manim_config (manimlib_bootstrap.py) builds camera = {frame_rate, pixel_height, pixel_width}, the old key names, plus directories, tex, text and log_level. Scene's camera already merges the right layer through _reference_camera_layer() (fm-5wq.44), and BackgroundRectangle now does too (fm-78b6). Any other reader of manim_config.camera.* or manim_config.vmobject.* gets {}.

The Reference reads the camera section in four places:
- scene.py:90, the camera merge;
- constants.py:13, DEFAULT_RESOLUTION;
- shape_matchers.py:60, BackgroundRectangle;
- extract_scene.py:78, fps.
The pinned corpus never reads manim_config directly, so user scenes on manimlib >= 1.7 are what this would break.

Work: build manim_config's camera section from _reference_camera_layer() (resolution, fps, background_color, background_opacity), keep the legacy keys if anything reads them, and add the pinned vmobject, mobject, colors, sizes and text sections that _pinned_manim_config() already carries, under any custom_config.yml overrides the native resolver supports. Then decide whether CLI flags (-c color, --fps, -r) update it, as the Reference's update_camera_config does.

Acceptance: a portal test reads manim_config.camera.background_color and .resolution under a temp cwd custom_config.yml and gets the Reference's values, and an absent config gives the pinned defaults.

